@@s001 Model equations: isolating w
@@s002 dato:what you know,ora:what you seek,ok:result
@@s003 dato:what you know,ora:the same move on both pans,ok:the weight found
@@s004 The equation is a balance in equilibrium: on the left 12, on the right w · 4. The point (x = 4, y = 12) in the model y = w · x.
@@s005 To free w one divides by 4. But <b>on both pans</b>, otherwise the balance tips over.
@@s006 On the right 4 ÷ 4 = 1 and w remains; on the left 12 ÷ 4 = 3. Nothing has "moved": the same operation has been done on both sides.
@@s007 Check: putting w = 3 back into the model, 3 · 4 = 12. The balance is still in equilibrium.
@@s008 dato:the equation,ora:the move on both sides,ok:w isolated
@@s009 dato:a feature (column),due:a point (row),ok:one equation
@@s010 dato:the known numbers,ora:the move on both sides,ok:w₁ found
@@s011 ora:unknown,dato:equation (one point),ok:solution
@@s012 dato:x (the data),due:w (the weights),ora:products,ok:y
@@s013 dato:the points,spento:possible lines,ok:the only line left
@@s014 Model equations: isolating w
@@s015 The same formula <span class="m">y = w &middot; x</span> is used in two opposite directions. Understanding in which of the two you are tells whether you must isolate <span class="m">w</span> or not. Then: how one really isolates it, what changes with more features, how many equations are needed, and why the model is an inner product. Exercise 4 of week 1 (Model Equations, Grasple).
@@s016 The two directions
@@s017 The model is always the same. What changes is <b>what you know</b> and <b>what you seek</b>.
@@s018 A &middot; Predicting (predict)
@@s019 You know <span class="m">w</span> and <span class="m">x</span>. You want <span class="m">y</span>.
@@s020 <b>You move nothing.</b> You substitute and calculate.
@@s021 This is what a model already trained does.
@@s022 B &middot; Learning (learn / fit)
@@s023 You know <span class="m">x</span> and <span class="m">y</span> (the data). You want <span class="m">w</span>.
@@s024 <b>Yes, you isolate <span class="m">w</span>.</b>
@@s025 This is what exercise 4 asks. And it is the definition of learning.
@@s026 The question to ask before moving anything is: <b>which is the only letter I do not know?</b> If it is y, you are in direction A and it is enough to substitute. If it is a w, you are in direction B and that letter must be isolated.
@@s027 The idea
@@s028 In this course <b>learning</b> is nothing but <b>solving the model equation for <span class="m">w</span></b>. Now with a division, then with <span class="m">Aw = y</span> and neural networks. The question never changes: <i>which weights make the model consistent with the data?</i>
@@s029 Doubt asked in chat: must I move the w to the other side?
@@s030 Yes, if the exercise gives you the data and asks for the weights — Model Equations is this case. No, if it gives you the weights and asks for the prediction.
@@s031 The same model, two directions
@@s032 Isolating w, one step at a time
@@s033 Model <span class="m">y = w &middot; x</span>. The datum is the point <span class="m">x = 4</span>, <span class="m">y = 12</span>. One writes the model, substitutes only the known numbers, and does on both sides the operation that "frees" w.
@@s034 Finding w from a point
@@s035 dato:what you already know,ora:the move now,ok:the weight found
@@s036 Write the model as it is. <span class="m">w</span> is the <b>unknown</b>: the only letter you do not know.
@@s037 Substitute <b>only</b> the numbers you know. <span class="m">w</span> stays a letter.
@@s038 Divide by 4 <b>on both sides</b>. On the right the 4 above and the 4 below simplify: that is the only purpose of the move.
@@s039 The weight is found. Always check by putting it back in: <span class="m">3 &middot; 4 = 12</span>. Correct.
@@s040 Why divide precisely by 4? Because in the model w is <b>multiplied</b> by 4, and the operation that cancels a multiplication is the division by the same number: 4 ÷ 4 = 1, and 1·w = w.
@@s041 The balance: the same move on both sides
@@s042 "Moving to the other side" does not exist
@@s043 It is a school saying, and it hides the true rule.
@@s044 The rule
@@s045 You move nothing: <b>you apply the same operation to the right and to the left</b>. The equation is a balance. If you take something from one pan you must take it from the other too, otherwise it tips over.
@@s046 For one step only it seems the same thing. The difference matters when several operations have been done on w. Each is cancelled by the <b>opposite operation</b>, and the operations are undone <b>in reverse order</b> with respect to how they were done: the last applied to w is the first to remove.
@@s047 If <span class="m">w</span> is...
@@s048 On <b>both</b> sides you do
@@s049 Example
@@s050 multiplied by 4
@@s051 divide by 4
@@s052 <span class="m">4w = 12</span> &rarr; <span class="m">w = 3</span>
@@s053 divided by 4
@@s054 multiply by 4
@@s055 <span class="m">w/4 = 3</span> &rarr; <span class="m">w = 12</span>
@@s056 added to 4
@@s057 subtract 4
@@s058 <span class="m">w + 4 = 12</span> &rarr; <span class="m">w = 8</span>
@@s059 subtracted 4
@@s060 add 4
@@s061 <span class="m">w - 4 = 12</span> &rarr; <span class="m">w = 16</span>
@@s062 At the exam, in English
@@s063 "Learning the model means <b>solving the model equation for the weights</b>: I isolate <span class="m">w</span> by applying the inverse operation to both sides."
@@s064 w / 4 + 2 = 5: two operations to undo
@@s065 Two inputs: x₁ and x₂ are columns, not points
@@s066 With more features the model becomes <span class="m">y = w<sub>1</sub>x<sub>1</sub> + w<sub>2</sub>x<sub>2</sub> + w<sub>3</sub></span>. The subscript of x often causes confusion.
@@s067 The subscript counts the columns, not the rows
@@s068 <span class="m">x<sub>1</sub></span> and <span class="m">x<sub>2</sub></span> are <b>not</b> two points: they are <b>two inputs of the same point</b> (two <i>features</i>). A point remains a point, so it gives <b>a single equation</b>. If you had two points you would have two rows of data, and then yes, two equations.
@@s069 When both indices are needed, the <b>bracketed superscript</b> is used for the point: x<sub>2</sub><sup>(3)</sup> is feature 2 of point 3. The brackets say that it is not a power.
@@s070 Two different indices
@@s071 The <b>bracketed superscript</b> numbers the <b>points</b> (the rows). The <b>subscript</b> numbers the <b>features</b> (the columns). Confusing them is the most common error.
@@s072 Doubt asked in chat: but I have x1 and x2
@@s073 The method does not change, only the count does. <span class="m">x<sub>1</sub></span> and <span class="m">x<sub>2</sub></span> are two inputs of the <b>same</b> point, so you always have <b>one</b> equation. Count the <span class="m">w</span> not given: if there is only one you isolate it reducing everything else to a number; if there are two or three you need a system with as many points.
@@s074 Rows, columns, equations
@@s075 Isolating a weight when the others are known
@@s076 Data: <span class="m">x<sub>1</sub> = 2</span>, <span class="m">x<sub>2</sub> = 3</span>, <span class="m">y = 20</span>, <span class="m">w<sub>2</sub> = 4</span>, <span class="m">w<sub>3</sub> = 1</span>. You seek <span class="m">w<sub>1</sub></span>. The unknowns are only the w that were not given: here one only.
@@s077 Isolating w1 with two inputs
@@s078 dato:what you already know,ora:the move now,ok:the weight found
@@s079 The model as it is.
@@s080 Substitute everything you know. One letter remains: you are back to the case with one input.
@@s081 Reduce to a number everything that does not contain <span class="m">w<sub>1</sub></span>: <span class="m">4&middot;3 + 1 = 13</span>.
@@s082 Subtract 13 from both sides: on the right the constant term disappears.
@@s083 Divide by 2 on both sides.
@@s084 Check: <span class="m">3.5&middot;2 + 4&middot;3 + 1 = 7 + 13 = 20</span>. Correct.
@@s085 The only trick
@@s086 As soon as you substitute the known numbers, the case with two inputs <b>collapses</b> into the case with one input. There is no new technique to learn: there is one more step of arithmetic.
@@s087 The same balance with two features
@@s088 All the w unknown: as many points as unknowns
@@s089 With <span class="m">w<sub>1</sub></span>, <span class="m">w<sub>2</sub></span>, <span class="m">w<sub>3</sub></span> all to be found you have <b>3 unknowns</b>, and one point gives <b>1 equation</b>. You isolate nothing: <b>3 points</b> are needed, which give a <b>system</b> of three equations.
@@s090 Count the letters you do not know
@@s091 One single unknown &rarr; you isolate. More than one &rarr; you need a system. The <span class="m">x</span> never count as unknowns: they are data. Only the <span class="m">w</span> that were not given count.
@@s092 What you are given
@@s093 Unknowns
@@s094 What you do
@@s095 all the <span class="m">w</span>, they ask for <span class="m">y</span>
@@s096 substitute and calculate, <b>you move nothing</b>
@@s097 all the <span class="m">w</span> except one
@@s098 <b>you isolate that one</b>
@@s099 no <span class="m">w</span>, only points
@@s100 a <b>system</b> is needed: as many points as the <span class="m">w</span>
@@s101 Three unknowns, one point at a time
@@s102 The model as inner product
@@s103 Put the <span class="m">x</span> of a point in one vector and the <span class="m">w</span> in another:
@@s104 The dot between two vectors is the <b>inner product</b> (<i>inner product</i>): you multiply the components in the same position and sum the products, w₁x₁ + w₂x₂. The result is a single number. It is the same <code>dot()</code> written in exercise 2 of week 1: for this reason "Model Equations" and "Inner Product" are in the same week, they are the same thing seen from two sides.
@@s105 Why it is convenient
@@s106 This writing <b>never changes</b>: with 2 features, with 50 (the poses of week 2) or with 784 (the pixels of an image), it remains <span class="m">y = w &middot; x + w<sub>3</sub></span>. What changes is only the length of the vectors. The inner product is explained in full in <a href="w02-prodotto-interno.html">inner product</a>.
@@s107 y = w · x + w₃ with the numbers above
@@s108 The affine model: with a single point, infinitely many lines
@@s109 If the model is <span class="m">y = w<sub>1</sub>x + w<sub>0</sub></span> you have <b>two</b> weights. With a single point you isolate nothing: one equation and two unknowns give <b>infinitely many</b> solutions, that is infinitely many lines all passing through that point.
@@s110 Count the equations
@@s111 You need <b>as many equations as unknowns</b>. Two weights &rarr; two points. Three weights &rarr; three points. The minimum number of points to learn a model is its number of parameters.
@@s112 With two points you obtain a <b>system of linear equations</b>, and there "moving" is no longer enough: matrices are needed. It is the road that leads to week 3 (systems and the inverse) and to week 5 (least squares, when the points are <i>more</i> than the parameters).
@@s113 Warning
@@s114 More points than parameters is not an error: it is the normal case in the real world. The system becomes <b>overdetermined</b> and in general has <b>no</b> exact solution. There one stops looking for the solution and looks for the <b>minimum error</b>. From here all the rest of the course arises.
@@s115 The text of the items is on Grasple (external site). In the course repo there is only the placeholder page <code>www/03-week/W01/04-Model_equations_pen_and_paper.html</code>.
@@s116 Lines through (1, 3): infinitely many, until a second point arrives
